By setting J˜z = Jy = −J˜⊥ , we solve for the ground state of
Ha in Eq. 3 of the main text with the Weiss mean-field theory.
The phase diagram is depicted in Fig. 3. The Weiss mean-field
theory should capture the physics of the ordered phase. As it
is expected, with large J˜⊥ and J2xz , the ground state is the
fragmented AFM. This qualitative result is actually consistent
with the result from the gauge mean-field theory in Fig. 1.
In the fragmented AFM, both S̃ x and S̃ z components develop uniform magnetic orders. The visible part of the order
is the S z component that is a linear combination of S̃ x and S̃ z .
As the z axis is defined locally on each sublattice, although the
⟨S z ⟩ is uniform, the overall state is an antiferromagnetic state.
Moreover, as some portion of the ordered moment remains
in the hidden octupolar part19,47,49 , this antiferromagnetic ordered state is referred as the “fragmented AFM”.
In this fragmented AFM, the reason for the presence of nonvanishing S̃ x and S̃ z order is due to the crossing coupling
J2xz . The S̃ z order, works as an effective and uniform Zeeman coupling to the S̃ x moment through the crossing coupling
J2xz . Once S̃ z develops an uniform order (for example from
the J⊥ coupling), it immediately polarizes S̃ x and generates a
S̃ x order even when there exists a nearest-neighbor antiferromagnetic S̃ x -S̃ x interaction.
